      "title": "Sketching a Line Using Two Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "1b239726-9ec0-5ecb-8b93-7c8edf0b2f3f",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following graphs shows the line $y=-4x?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We need to find two distinct points that lie on our line. Any two points will do!\n\n- For the first point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=0.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4x \\\\  & =-4(0) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the first point is $(0,0).$\n\n- For the second point, we will let the $x$-coordinate be $x=1.$ Then the $y$-coordinate is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =-4x \\\\  & =-4(1) \\\\  & =-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second point is $(1,-4).$\n\nFinally, we plot those two points on the $xy$-plane and draw a line through them. This is the graph of $y=-4x.$ \n\n![](images/8b/8b008b94eebbd24ae51670447848ce2d644c067d43dd92d72de85b048358cc54.png)",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
